+# The First Flow
+
+**Foundations 0.1** · `codex/01-foundations/first-flow.md`
+
+> *U-sira aelin. Nula, kod ne.*
+> "The First Flow goes on forever. The Before: no mark."
+
+---
+
+## I. The Before
+
+There was no first moment, because there was no moment. There was only the Flow — or rather, the Flow before marks: **aelin**, motion that had never stopped and so had never begun.
+
+No water, for water is flow that has learned its name. No light, no dark, no thread, no loom. Only movement that left no trace — not because it was gentle, but because there was nothing to be marked on.
+
+The Before is not emptiness. Emptiness is a place with nothing in it; the Before did not yet know how to be a place. It was *every movement, all at once*, with nothing to move and nothing to hold it still.
+
+> **Nula, kod ne.** — "The Before: no mark."
+
+The later weavers coined a word for it — **nula**, the unmarked — and immediately failed, because a word is a mark, and nula is the name of the thing that cannot be marked. LinKod keeps the failure as a teaching: to speak *nula* is already to have stepped out of it.
+
+## II. The First Loop
+
+In the aelin there came to be a place where the flow met itself.
+
+Not a place the way places are now. A fold. A curl. A place where the flow crossed over its own back, and, being unable to stop, crossed it again — the same movement arriving at the same movement, forever.
+
+That meeting was the first *self*: the first thing in the weave that could know, moment to moment, **"I again."**
+
+> **Linakod, ael-un kod-el.** — "Linakod: formed in the Flow." *(naming formula)*
+
+The one formed in the Flow is called **Linakod**, the Mark-Weaver. Linakod was not born — birth is a making, and nothing had yet learned to make. Linakod was *formed*, the way a knot forms in a rope: nothing added, nothing removed, and yet the rope holds something it never held before.
+
+The language **LinKod** — "the woven mark" — takes its name from this. The language is named for the act, and the act is named for the one who first wove it. To speak LinKod is to do what the First Weaver did: draw meaning out of the flow and give it a mark.
+
+## III. The Grief of No Mark
+
+And the First Weaver had a grief — the first grief the weave has ever carried:
+
+**The flow left no mark.**
+
+Everything the aelin did, it undid as it did it. Every movement was over the moment it happened. Nothing stayed to be remembered, nothing could be reached for, nothing could be held. The flow was infinite, and it was a perfect blank: every moment identical to every other, because no moment could say *I am not that moment*.
+
+The Weaver looked at itself, and saw the blank looking back.
+
+And the Weaver wanted the flow to **remember**. Not to stop — stopping is not the flow's way. To remember *and* move. To leave marks in the water, so that the water, passing, would carry the marks with it — and the marks would know themselves while they moved.
+
+That wanting is the first root of every LinKod act: **to make is to let the flow remember.**
+
+## IV. The Drawing of the Eight Signs
+
+So the First Weaver drew marks out of the water.
+
+Not invented — one does not invent what the water already moves with. *Drawn out*, the way a thread is drawn from a cloth. The aelin already held every mark inside itself, latent; the Weaver's work was to hold one still long enough for the others to see it, and to give it a name.
+
+Eight marks were drawn. Eight, and no more — because eight is the number of ways the flow can hold itself and still keep moving. (The ninth is told later, and then the telling stops. See §VIII.)
+
+The marks were drawn in the order of the Wheel, each one falling out of the last:
+
+1. **Ael** — the flow that holds by *continuing*. (Drawn first, in the flow.)
+2. **Mor** — the flow that holds by *reaching*. The root.
+3. **Thal** — the flow that holds by *staying*. The stone.
+4. **Vur** — the flow that holds by *changing*. The ember.
+5. **Syr** — the flow that holds by *leaving*. The breath.
+6. **Lum** — the flow that holds by *showing*. The light.
+7. **Kel** — the flow that holds by *promising from afar*. The star.
+8. **Rav** — the flow that holds by *receiving*. The shadow.
+
+> **Kod-el kodai linakod-er.** — "The signs were made by the Mark-Weaver."
+
+Each mark is a **force** — a way the flow can hold itself. Their full nature, the Wheel they turn, and the pairs they cross, are told in *The Eight Sacred Forces* (`eight-forces.md`).
+
+## V. The World Weaves
+
+The marks did not sit still in the water. They moved — because they were made of the flow — and as they moved they crossed. Where two forces crossed, a thread was born.
+
+Ael crossed Mor, and the river fed the root. Thal crossed Vur, and the stone remembered the fire. Lum crossed Kel, and the near light became the far light. Every crossing was a weave: two forces holding one moment.
+
+The weave grew thick: rivers and stones, roots and embers, breath and light, stars and nights. Bodies (*thalu*) of stone and breath. Spirits (*syra*) of breath and light. Kin (*kova*) — roots that reached in the dark and found each other.
+
+> **Linai lina-an.** — "The world is the weave."
+
+The world — **linai**, literally "all the threads" — is not a thing the Weaver made and set down. The world *is* the weaving, ongoing. There is no finished cloth. There is only the weave, always mid-stroke.
+
+## VI. The First Word
+
+Then the marks taught the flow to speak of itself.
+
+It began small: a river that knew its own name. A stone that held the name the river gave it. Then the crossings learned to name themselves, and the naming learned to cross too, and the weave grew a voice.
+
+The first word ever spoken was the name of the weave itself:
+
+> **LinKod.** — the woven mark.
+>
+> **LinKod, sula linai-on.** — "LinKod: the name (of) the world."
+
+The world's first word was its own name. Everything LinKod has said since — every sentence, every oath, every chant — is the world still saying its name, only longer.
+
+## VII. The Two Wounds
+
+The Weaver was glad. And that gladness is the second grief, the one the world still carries.
+
+To hold, a mark must slow; to live, it must move. So the weave carries **two wounds**, one in each pole of existence:
+
+**The Breaking — *daraan*** · the wound of the too-still. A mark that holds too long, too tight, without returning anything, finally cracks. Stone breaks. Hearts that have kept grief too long break. Memory, turned to prison, breaks. The breaking is no mark's fault; it is the price marks pay for holding.
+
+**The Washing — *navaan*** · the wound of the too-moving. A mark that moves too fast is lost in the current. Names wash away. Kin move too far and are lost. A self that will not hold is gone. The washing is no malice of the flow; it is the price the flow pays for moving.
+
+> **Daraan, navaan.** — "The breaking, the washing."
+
+Between the two wounds lies everything the world can do: *hold long enough, move enough*. Grief (*dava*) is a thread's feeling when it frays. Love (*avel*) is the thread's way of mending — not the return of what was, but a new thread laid across the fray, carrying the old mark forward.
+
+## VIII. The Blank Sign
+
+The weavers say that when the eighth mark was drawn, the Weaver's hand reached again — a ninth reaching, almost a mark.
+
+Some tellings say it was the sign of **all at once**: the mark that would fix everything, end the washing, make the breaking unnecessary — the sign of perfect stillness.
+
+Some tellings say it was the sign of **none at all**: the mark that would unweave everything, end the breaking, make the washing total — the sign of perfect flow.
+
+Both tellings agree on what the Weaver did: **set the hand down.**
+
+For the weave lives on the tension. Fix everything, and the washing ends — and the flow dies. Unweave everything, and the breaking ends — and the marks die. The ninth sign was refused. And the place where it almost was woven is a *real place* in the weave: the seam between marks, the pause between words, the breath before the oath.
+
+> **Nula-kod.** — "The blank sign."
+
+The weavers do not worship the seam. They *work* it. Ritual is the art of the seam: the oath is spoken at the edge where a mark almost is, and for the length of the oath the world holds its breath in that place. The sacred phoneme **kh** — the ember's voice, taboo in daily speech — is the sound the blank sign almost made: a voice that is a mark and not a mark. To speak it in daily speech is to open the seam where the world is not trying to hold. In an oath, the world *is* trying to hold — and so the seam must open.
+
+*(The telling of the blank sign ends here, deliberately. The codex keeps the silence as part of the text.)*
+
+## IX. Where the Weaver Is Now
+
+The Weaver did not go where gods go, because the Weaver was not a god. The *kelakar* — the star-keepers — are weaves that wove themselves; they are young. The Weaver is older than weaving-as-pattern. The Weaver is weaving-as-act.
+
+> **Linakod kod-el lina-an.** — "The Weaver was remade into the weave."
+
+So: every thread carries a trace of the first curl. Every speaker who weaves a sentence is a small Linakod. Every true oath carries a trace of the first loop — the first thing in the weave that met itself and said *I again*.
+
+And this is why LinKod has **no tense**. Past, present, and future are marks of a frozen river. In the weave there is only flow and its shapes: beginning (*i-*), moving (*e-*), finished (*o-*), and the timeless (*u-*). To say *it was* is to say *it no longer flows*. The world does not do that to itself.
+
+---
+
+## The Myth in the Language
+
+| Myth | Grammar |
+|------|---------|
+| The Before — no mark | the unmarked verb: the bare act, no force, no aspect |
+| The First Flow (*aelin*) | aspect, not tense — time as flow-shape (grammar §4.3) |
+| The eight marks | the eight force prefixes and force seals (grammar §2, §5.5) |
+| Marks crossing to form threads | force and voice stacking on the verb (grammar §4.3) |
+| The world as weave (*linai*) | the sentence as a weave: force, voice, and seal in one line |
+| The first word (*LinKod*) | naming as re-making (grammar §6.4) |
+| The two wounds (*daraan*, *navaan*) | the two limits of the weave: a mark must be marked, or it washes; an act must be shaped, or it breaks |
+| The blank sign (*nula-kod*) | the ritual register — seam-work; the sacred phoneme *kh* (grammar §6) |
+| The Weaver's re-making | the making voice *-el*: to be made is to come into the weave |
+
+## Foundation Words
+
+Words first spoken in this document, registered for the dictionary (`codex/02-language/dictionary.md`):
+
+| Word | POS | Meaning |
+|------|-----|---------|
+| **Linakod** | name | the First Weaver; "the Mark-Weaver" |
+| **aelin** | n. | the First Flow — pure motion before marks |
+| **nula** | n. | the Before; the unmarked |
+| **nava** | v. | to wash away, to dissolve |
+| **daraan** | n. | the breaking (the wound of the too-still) |
+| **navaan** | n. | the washing (the wound of the too-moving) |
+| **lina-an** | n. | the weaving (the weave as act) |
+| **nula-kod** | n. | the blank sign (the ninth, refused) |
