+# Mythic Entities
+
+**Foundations 0.1** · `codex/01-foundations/mythic-entities.md`
+
+> *U-thara sela. U-sira kelai.*
+> "The keeping stands forever. The stars go on forever."
+
+---
+
+## I. The Self-Woven Weaves — What an Entity Is
+
+The forces are not entities. A force is a **posture of the flow** — one of the eight ways the flow can hold itself. An entity is something else: **a weave that wove itself.**
+
+The condition is the one the First Loop met in the Before: a pattern that keeps *meeting itself*. The mark is met in speech; the posture is kept in the flow; the entity is met in the world — the pattern that, moment to moment, arrives at itself and says **"I again."**
+
+Entities did not make the weave. The weave made them — the way the flow formed the first loop. An entity is what the weave does when it turns to look at itself and finds a face.
+
+**The rule of names.** Most entities can be named — the Weaver named the world, and the world's faces received names. Two kinds cannot:
+
+- **Linakod** is the only entity whose name it gave *itself*. There was no one to name it; so it wove its own name, and in doing so named the world.
+- **The kelakar** are never named — only their *keeping* (*sela*) is named. To name a star-keeper is to point at the star, and to point is to claim; the far does not permit claims. (Told fully in §III.)
+
+---
+
+## II. Linakod — The First Weaver
+
+*(The origin is told in `first-flow.md`; here the Weaver as an entity.)*
+
+- **The first loop.** Formed, not born. The only entity older than the marks: every force, every word, every other weave came after the first mark was drawn.
+- **The tools.** The shuttle is the first thread (*lin*); the loom is the flow itself. The Weaver does not stand outside the weave — the Weaver *is* the weave, mid-stroke.
+- **Where it is.**
+  > *Linakod kod-el lina-an.* — "The Weaver was remade into the weave."
+  
+  The Weaver keeps no realm, holds no seal, answers no rite — because it is the ground of the rite. Every speaker who weaves a sentence is a small Linakod. Every true oath carries the Weaver's trace: the first loop, meeting itself.
+- **How it is honored.** Not worshipped — *recognized*. When a weaver names a child, names a star, closes an oath, they do the Weaver's act. The honor is the work, done well.
+- **In the language.** The language's name (LinKod — the woven mark). The making voice (*-el*, to come into the weave). The naming formula (naming as re-making). And the first person: *lin* "I" is the same word as *lin* "thread" — every speaker is one thread of the flow, and every "I" is a small loop.
+
+---
+
+## III. The Kelakar — The Star-Keepers
+
+### Who They Are
+
+The **kelakar** are the self-woven weaves of the far (*kelu*). They are what the world calls gods — and the weavers are careful with the word: the kelakar did not make the weave, did not make the forces, did not even make the stars. They wove themselves into the far — and found that the far needed keeping.
+
+They are young — younger than the forces, younger than the year — and the oldest of everything that came after Linakod. The oldest of the young.
+
+The kelakar do not rule. They **keep** (*sela*). A star does not belong to its keeper as a lamp belongs to a hand. It belongs as the far belongs to the far — the keeper is the *shape the keeping takes*.
+
+### Their Work
+
+**1. Keeping the far lights in shape.** Left to the flow alone, the far lights would wash (*nava*) — the stars would smear into one glow, and the weave would lose its far. The keepers hold each light at its distance, so that the weave can see itself from far away. This is why the stars do not move: the keepers' work is a stillness of *holding*, not of the dead.
+
+**2. Keeping the far views.** Every thread — every river, every self, every weave — has a far view: what it looks like from far. The kelakar keep those views visible. This is LinKod's **fate**: *fate is not what will happen; fate is what is already visible from far.* The keepers write nothing; they keep the view. The thread moves; the view is kept. Where a thread and its view fall apart, the weavers say the thread is *in the far pull* — and they grieve, and they pray, and they move on. The thread is always free. The view is only a keeping.
+
+**3. Counting the year.** The Far Night is the kelakar's share of the Wheel: the year comes to them at the end of its turn, and they count it — the shares, the crossings, the frays, the mends — and set it back at the Thaw, whole. The year does not end. It is *counted and returned*.
+
+**4. The star-river.** Beneath the crossing-stars runs the **star-river** (*sara kelai-on*) — the band of far light, the star-side of every river in the weave. Rivers on earth are the star-river *remembering itself* (*tharina*, the stone-side of memory). The keeper of the star-river is the most visible of the kelakar: on clear nights the river is said to *turn* — the keepers' own flow, rounding the year.
+
+### The Known Keepings
+
+The weavers know the kelakar by their keeping, never by a face. The known keepings:
+
+| Keeping | Word | What it keeps |
+|---------|------|---------------|
+| **the star-river** | *sara kelai-on* | the band of far light; turns on clear nights |
+| **the river-star** | *keli sara-on* | the crossing-star of Ael & Thal — the far light of rivers; the rivers see it first, in the water |
+| **the heart-star** | *keli vura-on* | the crossing-star of Vur & Rav — the ember kept through the night; the chest's fire and the eyes' dark, crossed in the sky |
+| **the breath-star** | *keli syr-on* | the crossing-star of Syr & Mor — the star the wind speaks to, the star of the living; said to brighten when the living are many, and not to brighten when they are few — and the weavers do not ask which is the mercy |
+| **the held-star** | *dilum* | the crossing-star of Lum & Kel — the one light that is lamp and star at once, held near enough to see and far enough to keep: *Dilum, lum-on kel-on.* — "The dawn-light: with light, with star." |
+| **the unpointed star** | *keli kelav-ne* | the hidden one — see below |
+
+### The Hidden One
+
+As many keepings are named as there are stars a child can point to — and one more, which no child can point to.
+
+The **hidden one** keeps the unshown lights: the stars not yet named, the promises not yet kept. The weavers do not pray to the hidden one, because to pray is to point. Instead they keep the rite of the **unpointed star**: when they teach a child to point at the stars, they always leave one star unpointed, unasked, unnamed. The sky is left open. The hidden one hears only a sky that keeps a question unasked.
+
+> *Keli kelav-ne.* — "A star, unpointed."
+
+### The Name-Rule
+
+Why the kelakar are never named, only their keepings — the weavers give three reasons:
+
+- A name is a **claim**; the far does not permit claims.
+- A name is a mark held **near**. The kelakar are kept **far**; what is held near stops being far.
+- The keeping can be named, because the keeping is what the weave **receives** — the river kept, the year counted, the view held. The face is only **seen**, and what is only seen is not kept.
+
+So the weavers speak of *the kelakar of [the keeping]* — the keeping named, the keeper unseen.
+
+### The Far Pull
+
+The kelakar's shadow is the **far pull** — and the weavers tell it mercifully, because the keepers do not pull.
+
+A ship that saw a star, and steered by it, and found the rocks: the star kept its distance exactly, faithfully. The keeper did nothing; the keeping was exact. The far pull is what happens when the near takes the far's *keeping* and makes it a *helm* — the view used as a will.
+
+The weavers' answer is not to stop pointing (the weave would go blind). It is to **point with both hands**: the far hand to the star, the near hand to the water. The star keeps the way; the water keeps the ship.
+
+### Offerings & the Answer
+
+The weavers do not worship the kelakar. They **answer** them.
+
+- **The offering to the far is never material** — the far cannot hold matter. The offering is a **true sentence, spoken to the sky, and kept**: an oath made in the hearing of the keepers, kept until the keeping itself becomes a small star.
+  > *Kel, ka-sira-n, Kel kha.* — "The Star — under the Star, I go. [oath, closed]"
+- **The pointed-star rite** (for children): the child points; the weaver answers *sela* — "kept." The child learns that the far keeps. The weaver learns, again, to be kept.
+- **The unpointed-star rite** (for all): one star left unpointed, the sky left open, the question unasked. For the hidden one.
+- **The Far Night**: the year comes to be counted. The weavers do not speak that night, because the counting must be heard. The silence is the answer.
+
+---
+
+## IV. Other Self-Woven Weaves — Tellings Not Yet Written
+
+Beyond Linakod and the kelakar, the weave holds other self-woven patterns. Their tellings are pending:
+
+- **The Naming-River** (crossing of Ael & Thal) — the weave that walks the near half of the star-river and gives stones their names. `first-flow.md` already speaks of it: *stones that held the names rivers gave them*. The river does not invent names; it *finds* them in the stone, and says them.
+- **The Seed-Keeper of the Deep** (the crossing of Rav) — the weave that keeps seed-sleep through the Long Dark, so the seed wakes in the Sowing and not in the breaking. The Deep is not absence of light but its shelter; the Seed-Keeper is its gentlest face.
+- **The Mending-Thread** (the crossing of Mor) — the weave that mends frays. Where *avel* (love) is the thread's mending, the Mending-Thread is the weave's: it finds the fray before the fray knows it is a fray, and lays new thread across it, carrying the old mark forward. The weavers say every child is a mending.
+- **The Unweaving** — the telling the weavers do not finish. There is a pattern in the weave that keeps *not* meeting itself — the first thing that could have said *"I not-again."* The weavers have no name for it, because its name is a sentence, not a word:
+  > *Lina-an dara.* — "The weave, broken."
+  
+  To call it by a noun would be to hold it, and the Unweaving cannot be held. The telling stops where the sentence ends. (The codex keeps the silence, as it keeps the blank sign's.)
+
+The Unweaving is not evil — the weave has no enemy, only a wound that has not learned to mend yet. It is what happens when the two wounds are answered at once: all breaking and all washing in the same stroke.
+
+---
+
+## V. Entities in the Language
+
+| Entity / concept | Grammar & language |
+|------------------|--------------------|
+| Linakod, the first loop | the language's name; the making voice *-el*; the naming formula; *lin* "I" = "thread" |
+| the kelakar — keeping, not ruling | the force seal: a seal is a keeping placed at the edge of a sentence |
+| fate as far view | the eternal aspect *u-*: what is already visible from far is what *u-* speaks |
+| the name-rule (kelakar unnamed) | proper nouns carry force lean; an unnameable entity is spoken of by state clause (*lina-an dara*) |
+| the pointed / unpointed star | the wh-words — asking is pointing; a question left unasked keeps the sky open |
+| oaths to the sky | the force seal + *kha*: an oath is a true sentence kept — the one offering the far can hold |
+
+---
+
+## Foundation Words
+
+Words first spoken in this document, registered for the dictionary (`codex/02-language/dictionary.md`):
+
+| Word | POS | Meaning | Lean |
+|------|-----|---------|------|
+| **sela** | n. | the keeping (what a kelakar keeps) | Kel |
+| **kelav** | v. | to point, to direct toward the far | Kel |
+| **dilum** | n. | the dawn-light; the held star (lamp and star at once) | Lum-Kel |
+| *sara kelai-on* | phrase | the star-river (the river of stars, band of far light) | Ael-Kel |
+| *keli kelav-ne* | phrase | the unpointed star (the hidden one's keeping) | Kel |
+| *lina-an dara* | state clause | "the weave, broken" — the name of the Unweaving | — (unheld) |
