+# Ritual Theory
+
+**Foundations 0.1** · `codex/01-foundations/ritual-theory.md`
+
+> *Ka-lina-n nisa-un.*
+> "I weave in the seam. [sacred speech]"
+
+---
+
+## I. What Ritual Is
+
+Ritual is not a second language. It is the same grammar at a different temperature — "one language, two temperatures" (grammar §1). The weaver who speaks the oath speaks the same words she speaks at the hearth; only the seam has opened.
+
+The blank sign was refused (`first-flow.md` §VIII), and the place where it almost was woven is a real place: the **seam** (*nisa*) — between any two marks, between any two words, between any two regions (`realms-cosmology.md`). Ritual is the art of the seam:
+
+> "The weavers do not worship the seam. They *work* it."
+
+Ritual has exactly **three verbs**, and every rite is those three in order:
+
+1. **to open** — *ka-*, the opening stitch
+2. **to hold** — the clause, in its ritual form
+3. **to close** — *kha*, the seal, or the held silence
+
+And one **condition**: ritual only works where the world is *trying to hold*. Daily speech is spoken where the world is not trying to hold — daily speech may trail off, may lie small, may leave a sentence unfinished. Ritual speech is spoken where the world is trying to hold. There, the rules are not manners. They are physics.
+
+---
+
+## II. The Three Movements
+
+### The Opening — *ka-*
+
+The sacred prefix lifts the sentence out of the Seen — the visible weave of daily speech — and into the seam. It is the first stitch: before it, the utterance is a conversation; after it, the utterance is a rite.
+
+What the opening does:
+
+- **declares the holding** — everything that follows, until the close, is under rite.
+- **sets initial stress** — ritual words take initial stress, daily words penultimate (grammar §3.3). The weight is at the front because a ritual sentence is held from the front, the way a knot is held.
+- **may be undone only by the close.** An opened sentence that is not closed is not a failed rite — it is a **hole**: a seam left open in the world. (Rule 1, §V.)
+
+### The Holding — the clause
+
+While the seam is open, the clause stands in its ritual form. The form depends on the rite — oath, naming, blessing, chant (§IV) — but all holdings share one property: **they must be complete.** A holding is a mark being laid, and a mark laid halfway is worse than none: it is a fray.
+
+### The Close — three kinds
+
+Every holding is closed in one of three ways:
+
+| Close | Form | Theory |
+|-------|------|--------|
+| **the active close** | *kha* — "so it is sealed" | the voice of the blank sign; the voice of the ember. The seam opens fully, the mark is laid, and the seam is stitched shut *by the unmade sign itself*. Used in oaths and strong namings. |
+| **the kept close** | the force seal — a force name at the sentence's end | the mark is not stitched shut but **held**: kept at the edge of the sentence by a force, visible, load-bearing. Used in blessings, chants, and elevated speech. |
+| **the resting close** | the held silence | no word at all. The weavers have no word for it, because a word is a mark, and the held silence is a mark's rest. Used only in the Far Night — the one rite that is not speech. |
+
+**The exception that proves the rule: Syr's open close.** A question sealed under the Breath is *never* closed — no *kha*, no seal:
+
+> *No sira vi, Syr.* — "Where do they go — under the Breath."
+
+The seam opens, the question is laid, and the sentence is **let go** instead of closed — "a question is not closed, it is let go" (*eight-forces.md*, Voices of the Forces). Every other rite closes. Only the question is released.
+
+### The Double Seal
+
+When a holding is **double-sealed** — the verb's force prefix names the same force as the seal — the mark is woven between two threads of the same force:
+
+> *Ael, ka-aelsiran, Ael kha.* — "The Flow — I flow under the Flow — under the Flow. [sealed]"
+
+A single-sealed oath is held from one side of the seam; a double-sealed one is held **from both sides**, and there is no room between for the blank sign to pass. That is why a double-sealed oath "may not be broken" — not because it is stronger, but because the breaking has no seam to pass through.
+
+The classic oath *Ael, ka-siran, Ael kha.* is a **force-oath** — sworn to the force itself — but single-sealed (the verb carries no prefix): it can bend under enough years. The double-sealed form does not bend.
+
+### The Sacred Phoneme — *kh*
+
+The full theory of the taboo sound (grammar §3.1, `first-flow.md` §VIII):
+
+- *kh* [x] is "the sound the blank sign almost made: a voice that is a mark and not a mark."
+- **In daily speech, kh is taboo.** To speak it where the world is not trying to hold is to open the seam where it should stay shut. A stray *kh* is a small tear — and it is mended the way all frays are mended: by saying the word again, without it.
+- **In ritual, kh is required where the seam must open fully** — in the active close (*kha*) and in the force-oaths that carry it. It is **of right** in Vur: the ember's voice, the only force whose seals are *kh*-carried as a matter of course (*eight-forces.md*).
+- The paradox is deliberate: the most forbidden sound in the language is also the most powerful, because it is the sound of the *unmade* mark. To voice it is to let the ninth sign exist, briefly, for a syllable. The weavers do not fear the sound. They ration it.
+- The full list of *kh*-words (beyond *kha*) is held for the mythic lexicon (`02-language/lexicon/mythic.md`) — pending.
+
+---
+
+## III. The Signature Forms
+
+### The Oath — the Inversion
+
+The oath inverts the clause: the object stands first, the verb is sacred, the subject is postponed, and the close seals it (grammar §6.2):
+
+```
+OBJECT,  ka-VERB-SUBJECT-clitic,  FORCE [kha].
+```
+
+> *Talu, ka-koda-n, Ael.* — "The house — I make it — under the Flow."
+> *Ael, ka-siran, Ael kha.* — "The Flow — under the Flow, I go. [oath, sealed]"
+
+**Why the inversion:** in a daily sentence the deed leads (VSO — the deed before the doer); in an oath the **thing** leads. An oath is not about the act; it is about the thing. The swearer stands *after* the thing — postponed, small, its servant for the length of the holding. *The subject is postponed because the oath makes the swearer small.*
+
+The **oath to the sky** is the oath's far form — the offering to the kelakar, "a true sentence, spoken to the sky, and kept" (`mythic-entities.md`):
+
+> *Kel, ka-sira-n, Kel kha.* — "The Star — under the Star, I go. [oath, sealed]"
+
+### The Naming — the Re-Making
+
+The naming formula is the making voice, because to name is to make over (grammar §6.4):
+
+```
+FORCE,  NAME  kod-el  PREDICATE,  FORCE.
+```
+
+> *Lum, Vela kod-el luma, Lum.* — "Lum — Vela is re-made 'luminous' — under the Light."
+> *Mor, Tavi kod-el mora, Mor.* — "Mor — Tavi is re-made 'rooted' — under the Root." (*Tavi* here is a name — see the custom below.)
+
+**The first-name custom.** The first child of a house takes a word of the house's work as its name: a weaver's child is named *Lina*, a stone-shaper's child *Thala*, a root-tender's child *Tavi*. The child is named under the house's force — the work, inherited as a mark before the child has spoken a word.
+
+### The Chant — the Reduplication
+
+The chant is the rite of the sustained holding: the verb is repeated, and the repetition *is* the rite (grammar §6.3):
+
+- **full reduplication** — the act sustained and repeated: *morav morav* "growing upon growing"
+- **partial reduplication** — the act's rhythm: *mo-morav* "growing, in measure"
+
+> *Morav morav, lumavn kelai, Kel.* — "Growing upon growing, I bless the stars — under the Star."
+
+**The theory:** reduplication is the chant's way of *dwelling in the seam*. A daily sentence lays a thread once; a chant lays the same thread again and again, each pass thickening the weave a little. A chant does not say a thing. A chant *weaves* a thing.
+
+**The last-pass rule.** A chant is *bounded*: it knows its final pass. An unbounded repetition is not a chant — it is the Unweaving wearing a sound. Weavers end a chant the way they end everything in the weave: cleanly, at the edge, with the seal.
+
+---
+
+## IV. The Rites
+
+Catalogue of the standing rites — place, form, and theory. (Full ceremonial texts: planned for `04-culture/ceremonies/`.)
+
+### 1. The Oath
+
+- **Where:** the riverbank — the most beloved crossing (Ael & Thal: the River); the offering to the sky stands at the horizon.
+- **Why there:** an oath is a mark asked of the flow to remember it; it must be spoken where the asking can be heard by both (`realms-cosmology.md`, the Geography of Ritual).
+- **Form:** `OBJECT, ka-VERB-SUBJECT, FORCE [kha]`; double-sealed when the verb's force matches the seal.
+- **The Rav variant:** an oath in Rav is a *promise of silence* — spoken almost not at all, the seal whispered, and *kha* dropped: the only oath-form that forgos the active close, "for the dark is kept, not announced":
+  > *Rav, ka-ravai-n, Rav.* — "Rav — I rest. [under Rav]" (whispered)
+
+### 2. The Naming
+
+- **Where:** the threshold of the house — the house is the Seen-side; the Within is what is being named.
+- **Form:** `FORCE, NAME kod-el PREDICATE, FORCE`.
+- **Custom:** the first name is a word of the work (§III).
+
+### 3. The Blessing
+
+- **Where:** anywhere the light reaches — Lum is the mobile force, the only one whose rite has no fixed edge.
+- **Form:** a force-prefixed verb and a seal, optionally *ka-* in ceremony — a lighter opening, "the seam opened only as far as the light reaches":
+  > *Ka-lumav-n talu, Lum.* — "I bless the house — under the Light. [sacred speech]"
+
+### 4. The Chant
+
+- **Where:** any seam — the chant walks the edge.
+- **Form:** reduplication and a seal; the last-pass rule.
+
+### 5. The Pointed-Star Rite
+
+- **For:** children, on a clear night.
+- **Form:** the child points (*kelav*); the weaver answers one word — *sela*, "kept."
+- **Theory:** the child learns that the far keeps; the weaver re-learns, again, to be kept (`mythic-entities.md`).
+
+### 6. The Unpointed-Star Rite
+
+- **For:** all, on a clear night.
+- **Form:** one star left unpointed, unasked, unnamed. The sky is left open.
+  > *Keli kelav-ne.* — "A star, unpointed."
+- **Theory:** the hidden one "hears only a sky that keeps a question unasked." This rite is the unpointed half of the pointed rite: the same night, the same sky, two halves of one answer.
+
+### 7. The Far Night
+
+- **When:** the Far Night's share of the year, when the year comes to be counted.
+- **Form:** none. The weavers do not speak that night, "because the counting must be heard. The silence is the answer."
+- **Theory:** the opposite of ritual speech is not noise; it is held silence. The Far Night is the seam *closed* — everything held, nothing said. The year is mended in the counting: its frays are seen from the far, and set back whole at the Thaw.
+
+### 8. The Rest-Rite
+
+- **Where:** the Deep — night, forest.
+- **Form:** one word of the Deep, said once, low — *rav.* or *mora.*
+- **Theory:** the quietest edge of ritual: not an asking but a keeping-company. The rest-rite asks nothing of the Deep; it tells the Deep it is not alone.
+
+### 9. The Thin-Place Healing *(working form)*
+
+- **Where:** the thin place itself — where the weave shows through.
+- **Form (working, pending the ceremonies document):** the healer stands where the weave is thin, names the missing force, gives it one true act, and seals it:
+  > *Ael, sira-n, Ael.* — "Ael — I go — under Ael." (for a world missing the Flow)
+- **Theory:** "give the missing force its name, and the weave thickens back" (`realms-cosmology.md`, Open Question 3). A thin place is not filled with a new thread; it is *remembered back* — the force named, and the weave, which never really lost the thread, re-ties it.
+
+---
+
+## V. The Rules of Ritual Speech
+
+The codified list — what a weaver may and may not do where the world is trying to hold:
+
+1. **A ritual sentence must close** — with *kha*, with the seal, or with the held silence of the Far Night. An opened sentence that is not closed is a hole.
+2. **Daily speech never carries *kh*.** A stray *kh* is a small tear, mended by saying the word again without it.
+3. **Rites stand at edges.** The oath at a water-edge — or the horizon, for the offering to the far; the naming at a threshold; the blessing wherever the light reaches; the chant on any seam. A rite spoken in the middle of a region does not take — the middle of a region is still; the edge of a region is where marks are laid.
+4. **A double-sealed oath may not be broken and may not be loosened.** Held from both sides, it is not renegotiable.
+5. **A question (Syr) is never *kha*-closed.** "A question is not closed, it is let go."
+6. **A chant is bounded.** It knows its last pass. An unbounded repetition is not a chant.
+7. **The seal must be the force that governs the holding.** A seal under the wrong force is a **false address** — a mark laid in the wrong region (`realms-cosmology.md`): the sentence does not bind. It simply does not hold.
+8. **Ritual words take initial stress; daily words take penultimate.** The temperature is audible.
+
+---
+
+## VI. Ritual and the Two Wounds
+
+Ritual is the weave's standing answer to its two wounds (`first-flow.md` §VII):
+
+- **Against the washing — the oath.** The washing takes the unmarked; an oath is a mark asked of the flow to remember it. A true sentence spoken to the sky is a thread with a seal on it, and the current does not take what is held.
+- **Against the breaking — the double seal.** A single-sealed oath can break under enough years; a double-sealed one does not, because the breaking has no seam to pass through.
+- **The mending — the naming.** Every naming is a mending at the fray of a self's beginning: the child is a fray in the weave, and the naming lays the new thread across it, "carrying the old mark forward."
+- **The year's mending — the Far Night.** The year is counted and returned: its frays seen from the far, set back whole at the Thaw.
+- **The warning — no rite of the letting-go.** The codex has rites for every holding and **no rite for the unholding**. To use ritual to unweave is to reach for the blank sign, and the weavers have a name for the attempt (*lina-an dara*) but no rite for it. If a rite of the letting-go is ever told, it will be told last.
+
+---
+
+## VII. Ritual in the Language
+
+| Ritual element | Theory | Computation (`03-computation`) |
+|----------------|--------|-------------------------------|
+| *ka-* | the opening stitch; the seam opens | enter a ceremony block |
+| the holding | the clause, complete | the act — precondition, body, witness |
+| *kha* | the active close | commit with full witness |
+| the force seal | the kept close | commit under a force (transaction boundary) |
+| the double seal | held from both sides | atomic, non-negotiable contract |
+| reduplication | the thread laid again | bounded loop |
+| the open close (Syr) | the question let go | a query with no resolution — left open by design |
+| the Far Night | the held silence | a no-op block: the system holds; nothing executes |
+| the thin-place healing | naming the missing force | initializing a missing namespace with its seal |
+
+A LinKod program that uses the ritual register is a program with seams: the *ka-* blocks are where the computation tries to hold, and the interpreter — like the weavers — either closes them, or calls them holes.
+
+---
+
+## VIII. Open Questions
+
+1. **The *kh*-wordlist.** Beyond *kha*, the grammar promises "a few others." The full list is held for the mythic lexicon — pending.
+2. **Can a double-sealed oath be answered?** The weavers' instinct: two double seals may not cross — "the seam does not open twice in the same place." Or can it? A countervailing oath is the codex's great untold legal myth.
+3. **The thin-place healing form** needs the ceremonies document to settle it (working form now, §IV.9).
+4. **A rite of the letting-go.** The codex has no rite for the unholding. That may be by nature — or it may be that it is simply not told yet.
+
+*(Ritual theory ends at the Far Night: the counting must be heard.)*
+
+---
+
+## Foundation Words
+
+This document adds **no words** — and that is the point. The ritual register is not a second language; it is the same grammar at a different temperature, and its entire vocabulary already exists: *ka-*, *kha*, the eight force names, *sela*, *nisa*, *korai*, *tura*, *sula* — and the same roots spoken daily. The weavers say it plainly: a second language would be a second weave, and the weave is one.
