+# LinKod — Core Grammar
+
+**Codex 0.1 · First Draft** · `codex/02-language/grammar.md`
+
+> *Before the marks there was the Flow. The First Weaver drew signs out of the water, and the signs taught the world to speak of itself.*
+> — Opening fragment of the First Flow (full text planned for `codex/01-foundations/first-flow.md`)
+
+**LinKod** (Lin·kod) — "the woven mark". From *lina* "to weave, to flow through" + *kod* "sign, mark, seal".
+
+LinKod is a mythic language: a system in which nature, emotion, ritual, and creation are not metaphors but **grammar**. Every act is done under one of the Eight Sacred Forces; time is spoken as flow; and the ceremonial register is the same grammar run at ritual heat.
+
+This document defines the linguistic core: phonology, morphology, syntax, ritual syntax, and worked examples. Cosmology lives in `codex/01-foundations/`; the growing wordlist lives in `codex/02-language/dictionary.md`.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Design Principles
+
+1. **Flow over tense.** LinKod does not mark absolute tense (past/present/future). Time is expressed through *aspect* — the shape of an act's flow — because in LinKod cosmology the First Flow is the only real time.
+2. **Force-driven semantics.** Every act is done under one of the Eight Sacred Forces. Force is a *grammatical element* (a verb prefix and a sentence seal), not a figure of speech: *how* an act is done is part of what is said.
+3. **Doing moves; being stays.** Action clauses are VSO — the deed leads. State clauses have no verb at all: subject + predicate, like a stone. (See §5.2.)
+4. **One language, two temperatures.** The ritual register is not a second language. It is the same grammar with sacred prefix, inversion, and seal — heat applied to the same engine.
+
+---
+
+## 2. The Eight Sacred Forces
+
+(Condensed. Full cosmology, histories, and entities: `codex/01-foundations/`.)
+
+| Force | Name | Domains | Prefix |
+|-------|------|---------|--------|
+| The Flow | **Ael**  | water, time, change, beginnings | `ael-` |
+| The Ember | **Vur**  | fire, passion, transformation, wrath | `vur-` |
+| The Stone | **Thal** | earth, endurance, memory, body | `th-`  |
+| The Breath | **Syr** | wind, thought, freedom, voice | `syr-` |
+| The Light | **Lum** | truth, spirit, vision, blessing | `lum-` |
+| The Root | **Mor**  | growth, kinship, nurture, seed | `mor-` |
+| The Shadow | **Rav** | dream, rest, mystery, night | `rav-` |
+| The Star | **Kel**  | fate, distance, cosmos, awe | `kel-` |
+
+Every verb may carry **one** force prefix. A verb with no force prefix is *unmarked* — the act is done under no named force, or the force is left to context.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Phonology
+
+### 3.1 Consonants (20)
+
+|  | Bilabial | Alveolar | Palatal | Velar | Uvular | Glottal |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Stops | p b | t d | — | k g | q | — |
+| Nasals | m | n | — | — | — | — |
+| Fricatives | f v | s z | sh (ʃ) | — | — | h |
+| Approximants | w | r | j (y) | — | — | — |
+| Laterals | — | l | — | — | — | — |
+
+**The sacred phoneme `kh` [x].** A voiced uvular fricative that occurs **only** in ritual words and oaths (*kha*, and a few others). In everyday speech the sound is taboo; in ritual speech it is the voice of the ember. (See §6.)
+
+### 3.2 Vowels
+
+- Five monophthongs: **a e i o u** ([a e i o u], all open).
+- Four diphthongs: **ai ei oi au** (nucleus + glide, one syllable).
+- No phonemic length.
+
+### 3.3 Syllables & Stress
+
+- Syllable shape: **(C)(C)V(C)**.
+  - Onset clusters: stop + (l, r) only — *pl-, pr-, kl-, kr-, tl-, tr-*.
+  - Codas: *m n r s l h*.
+- Stress: **penultimate** by default. Exceptions:
+  - words ending in a diphthong take **final** stress (*kELai*);
+  - ritual words take **initial** stress (*KORai*).
+- Elision: an unstressed final vowel before an initial vowel drops in rapid speech, marked with an apostrophe: *talu uma* → *talu'uma*.
+
+### 3.4 Orthography
+
+- Latin script, one letter per sound. `j` = [j] (y as in "yes"); `sh` = [ʃ]; `kh` = [x] (ritual only); `q` = [q] (rare, archaic/loan).
+- Hyphens mark prefix attachment in a word's **first attestation**; in running text, prefixes write flush (*lumavora* for *luma-vora*, "to speak truly").
+- Capitalization: sentence-initial, force names, and personal names.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Morphology
+
+### 4.1 Nouns
+
+LinKod nouns have **no grammatical gender**. Every noun has a *force lean* — the cultural expectation of which force it belongs to (river → Ael, stone → Thal). Lean is semantic, not grammatical: any noun may be used under any force.
+
+**Number.** Singular = zero. Plural = **-ai**.
+A root ending in a vowel fuses with the plural: *keli* "star" → *kelai* "stars"; *talu* "house" → *taluai* "houses".
+
+**Cases** (suffixes, applied after derivational suffixes):
+
+| Suffix | Function | Example |
+|--------|----------|---------|
+| **-un** | locative (in / at / on) | *sarun* "on the river" |
+| **-is** | ablative (from / off) | *thalais* "from the stone" |
+| **-at** | allative (toward) | *taluat* "toward the house" |
+| **-er** | instrumental (by means of) | *koder* "by mark/sign" |
+| **-on** | comitative (with) | *kovaon* "with kin" |
+| **-ai** | plural (see fusion rule) | *taluai* "houses" |
+
+**Derivation** (applies to noun, verb, and adjective roots):
+
+| Suffix | Function | Example |
+|--------|----------|---------|
+| **-kar** | agent, doer | *lina-kar* "weaver" |
+| **-en**  | place, home of | *luma-en* "light-sanctuary" |
+| **-um**  | diminutive | *vurum* "little fire, spark" |
+| **-os**  | augmentative | *saros* "great river, sea" |
+| **-an**  | abstract nominalizer | *sura* "calm (adj.)" → *suraan* "calm (the state)" |
+
+Order of suffixing: **root → derivational → case/number**.
+Example: *lina-kar-ai-un* — "in the weavers" (root *lina*, agent *-kar*, plural *-ai*, locative *-un*).
+
+### 4.2 Adjectives
+
+Adjectives are **invariable** (no number, no agreement) and **precede** the noun:
+
+- *turu talu* — "beautiful house"
+
+An adjective may take a force prefix when the quality is cast under a force:
+
+- *lumturu talu* — "truly beautiful (in the light) house"
+- *ravturu* — "beautiful as in a dream"
+
+Predicative adjectives form copula-less state clauses (§5.2).
+
+### 4.3 Verbs
+
+The verb is the center of LinKod. Full shape:
+
+```
+[FORCE] [ASPECT] ROOT [-el] [PERSON]
+  ael-    i-     sira     -el      -n
+```
+
+**Force prefix** (§2): the full force name — `ael- vur- th- syr- lum- mor- rav- kel-`.
+
+**Aspect prefixes:**
+
+| Prefix | Aspect | Sense |
+|--------|--------|-------|
+| **i-** | inceptive | the act begins |
+| **e-** | continuous | the act in motion |
+| **o-** | completive | the act finished; the result stands |
+| **u-** | eternal / habitual | the act repeated, or timeless (truths, ritual) |
+| **∅**  | aorist | the bare act, no flow-shape (default) |
+
+Aspect stacks **after** force: *vur-o-koda* "shaped in the fire (already)".
+
+**Voice.**
+- **Active** (default): the doer is the subject.
+- **Making voice -el:** the patient becomes subject; the act is presented as something coming into being: *kod-el* "is made / comes into being / is born".
+- Force + *-el* = passive-by-force: *vurkod-el* "is made by fire (burned into being)".
+- When a making-voice agent is expressed, it takes the instrumental **-er** ("by ~"): *kod-el kodai linakod-er* — "the signs were made by the Mark-Weaver".
+
+**Person clitics** (postposed to the verb):
+
+| Person | Clitic |
+|--------|--------|
+| 1sg | **-n** |
+| 2sg | **-ta** |
+| 3sg | **-ri** |
+| 1pl | **-si** |
+| 2pl | **-ki** |
+| 3pl | **-vi** |
+
+Person clitics are **optional** when the subject is an overt full pronoun. LinKod prefers the light clitic; the full pronoun is used for emphasis or topicalization.
+
+### 4.4 Pronouns & Demonstratives
+
+|  | 1sg | 2sg | 3sg | 1pl | 2pl | 3pl |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Full | **lin** | **ta** | **ri** | **si** | **ki** | **vi** |
+| Possessive (post-noun) | **na** | **ta** | **ri** | **si** | **ki** | **vi** |
+
+- LinKod has **one** third-person pronoun (*ri*) — no grammatical gender; animate vs. neutral is resolved by context and the noun's force lean.
+- *lin* "I" is a deliberate homonym of *lin* "thread, woven line" — "I am one thread of the Flow". (See the language's name, §1.)
+- Demonstratives (post-noun): **ni** "this, near" · **vo** "that, far".
+
+---
+
+## 5. Syntax
+
+### 5.1 Action clauses: VSO
+
+The deed leads. Base order:
+
+```
+[PARTICLES]  VERB+clitics  SUBJECT  (ADJ)  OBJECT+modifiers  ADVERB
+```
+
+- *Koda-n talu ni.* — "I make this house."
+- *Va koda talu?* — "Who makes the house?"
+- *Sira vi no?* — "Where do they go?"
+
+**Negation:** particle *ne* before the verb. *Ne koda-n talu.* — "I do not make the house."
+
+**Yes/no questions:** particle *ha* sentence-initial. *Ha koda-n talu?* — "Do I make the house?"
+
+**Wh-words** take the slot of the thing asked about; word order otherwise unchanged:
+
+| Word | Meaning |
+|------|---------|
+| **tha** | what |
+| **va**  | who |
+| **kwa** | which |
+| **no**  | where |
+| **lu**  | when |
+| **is**  | how |
+| **ya**  | why |
+| **ve**  | how much / how many |
+
+### 5.2 State clauses: SP, no verb
+
+"X is Y" takes **no copula**: the subject stands, and the predicate (adjective, noun, or nominal phrase) follows. A state clause is like a stone — nothing in it moves.
+
+- *Talu turu.* — "The house is beautiful."
+- *Lin lina-kar.* — "I am a weaver."
+- *Syra rav.* — "The spirit is at rest." (*rav* "shadow" as predicate = "in shadow")
+
+State clauses are timeless by nature; a force seal may be appended for emphasis (§5.5).
+
+### 5.3 Relative clauses
+
+Noun + relativizer **e** + embedded VSO clause:
+
+- *Talu e vi-er kod-el, turu.* — "The house (that) they made is beautiful."
+- *Lina-karai e maru si, morav.* — "The weavers (whom) we carry grow."
+
+### 5.4 Topic & emphasis
+
+A fronted topic takes a postposed **ha**:
+
+- *Talu vo ha, koda-n.* — "As for that house, I made it."
+
+### 5.5 The Force Seal
+
+In ritual or elevated register, the governing force's **name is appended to the sentence as a seal** — the period of a LinKod sentence can be a force:
+
+- *Aelisiran sarun, Ael.* — "I began to flow along the river — under the Flow."
+
+---
+
+## 6. The Ritual Register
+
+Ceremonial forms used in oaths, chants, blessings, and namings. The same grammar, at ritual heat. Full theory — the seam, the three closes, the double seal, the *kh* taboo, the standing rites: `codex/01-foundations/ritual-theory.md`.
+
+### 6.1 Sacred speech: *ka-*
+
+The prefix **ka-** on the first verb of a ritual utterance marks the whole utterance as sacred speech. In a *ka-* construction the verb takes initial stress, and the sacred phoneme *kh* may appear in ritual words.
+
+- *Ka-koda-n talu ni.* — "In sacred speech: I make this house."
+
+### 6.2 The Oath (binding seal)
+
+The oath inverts the clause: the **object stands first** (what is sworn to, before the swearer), the verb is sacred, the subject is postponed, and the sentence is sealed with a force name:
+
+```
+OBJECT,  ka-VERB-SUBJECT-clitic,  FORCE.
+```
+
+- *Ael, ka-siran, Ael.* — "The Flow — under the Flow, I go. [oath]"
+- When seal and force prefix name the **same** force, the oath is **double-sealed**: it may not be broken.
+
+The oath is closed with **kha** — the sacred-phoneme particle, "so it is sealed":
+
+- *Ael, ka-siran, Ael kha.*
+
+### 6.3 Chant reduplication
+
+- **Full reduplication** of the verb root = the act sustained and repeated, ceremonially: *morav morav* "growing upon growing".
+- **Partial reduplication** (repeated initial syllable) = the act's rhythm: *mo-morav* "growing, in measure".
+- Chants close with a force seal, often with *kha*.
+
+### 6.4 Naming formula
+
+Naming is **re-making**: a naming formula uses the making voice *-el*, because to name is to make a person over.
+
+```
+FORCE,  NAME  kod-el  PREDICATE,  FORCE.
+```
+
+- *Lum, Vela kod-el luma, Lum.* — "Lum — Vela is re-made 'luminous' — under the Light."
+
+---
+
+## 7. Worked Examples
+
+| # | Sentence | Interlinear gloss | Translation | Note |
+|---|----------|-------------------|-------------|------|
+| 1 | *Koda-n talu ni.* | koda-n ‖ talu ‖ ni — make-1SG ‖ house ‖ DEM.NEAR | "I make this house." | base VSO |
+| 2 | *Ne luma-vora-n.* | ne ‖ lum-a-vora-n — NEG ‖ LIGHT-speak-1SG | "I do not speak brightly — I do not speak truth." | force prefix recasts the verb |
+| 3 | *Sira vi no?* | sira ‖ vi ‖ no — go ‖ 3PL ‖ WHERE | "Where do they go?" | wh-slot |
+| 4 | *Ha o-sira ri?* | ha ‖ o-sira ‖ ri — Q ‖ PERF-go ‖ 3SG | "Has she/he/it arrived?" | completive + question |
+| 5 | *Talu turu, syra rav.* | talu ‖ turu, ‖ syra ‖ rav — house ‖ beautiful, ‖ spirit ‖ in-shadow | "The house is beautiful; its spirit rests." | two state clauses, zero verbs |
+| 6 | *Lin lina-kar linai-un.* | lin ‖ lina-kar ‖ linai-un — I ‖ thread-weaver ‖ world-LOC | "I am a weaver in the world." | *linai* "the world" = "all threads" |
+| 7 | *Aelisiran sarun, Ael.* | ael-i-sira-n ‖ sara-un ‖ Ael — FLOW-INCEPT-go-1SG ‖ river-LOC ‖ FLOW(seal) | "I began to flow along the river — under the Flow." | force + aspect written flush; seal |
+| 8 | *Talu e vi-er kod-el, turu.* | talu ‖ e ‖ vi-er kod-el ‖ turu — house ‖ REL ‖ BY-3PL make-MAKING ‖ beautiful | "The house they made is beautiful." | relative + making voice |
+| 9 | *Ael, ka-siran, Ael kha.* | Ael ‖ ka-sira-n ‖ Ael kha — FLOW ‖ SACRED-go-1SG ‖ FLOW SEALED | "The Flow — under the Flow, I go. [oath, closed]" | oath pattern |
+| 10 | *Morav morav, lumavn kelai, Kel.* | morav morav ‖ lum-a-v-n ‖ kelai ‖ Kel — grow-grow ‖ LIGHT-bless-1SG ‖ star-PL ‖ STAR(seal) | "Growing upon growing, I bless the stars — under the Star." | chant: full reduplication |
+
+---
+
+## 8. Writing System
+
+- **Primary script:** the Latin orthography of §3.4.
+- **Kod-script** (the mark-writing) is in development — planned for `codex/04-culture/glyphs/`. Design intent: every glyph is a **single continuous stroke** (one-line writing), mirroring *lin* "thread"; stroke direction encodes the glyph's force lean (Flow strokes travel clockwise, Stone strokes fall, Star strokes radiate outward). Full specification pending.
+
+---
+
+## 9. Computational Notes
+
+LinKod's grammar is designed to map one-to-one onto the LinKod interpreter (blueprint: `codex/03-computation/interpreter.md`):
+
+| Grammar | Computation |
+|---------|-------------|
+| force prefix (`ael-`, `vur-`, …) | the act namespaced under a force |
+| aspect (`i- e- o- u-`) | execution phase: begin / loop / commit / invariant |
+| person clitics | the acting context (who/what runs the act) |
+| making voice `-el` | values coming into being (constructors) |
+| state clause (SP, no verb) | declarations (no side effects) |
+| ritual `ka-` | ceremony blocks (validated, sealed scopes) |
+| oath (O, ka-V-S, FORCE) | contracts: precondition, act, sealed postcondition |
+| force seal | transaction boundary markers |
+| chant reduplication | bounded repetition (loops) |
+
+A LinKod program is a set of sentences; the interpreter executes them the way a speaker utters them — the deed before the doer.
+
+---
+
+## 10. Open Questions & Roadmap
+
+**Open questions** (to resolve in the next draft):
+
+1. Keep *ne* / *ha* as separate particles, or absorb them as verb prefixes?
+2. Mark force lean in the dictionary with a per-entry tag, or leave it to prose notes?
+3. Is a second plural (kin-specific) needed, or is *-ai* enough for now?
+4. Sacred phoneme: keep the *kh*-wordlist minimal, or extend it?
+5. Diphthong *ei* is unused in the starter lexicon — keep it in the inventory?
+
+**Roadmap:** `01-foundations` (First Flow, forces, entities, realms) → lexicon expansion (nature, emotion, social, technology, mythic) → `03-computation` (interpreter blueprint, formal ritual syntax, example programs) → `04-culture` (myths, chants, ceremonies, glyph spec).
